<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antigua and Barbuda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antigua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a> is an archipelagic country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> made up of Antigua, Barbuda and dozens of smaller islands. Hunter-gatherers settled the islands long before European contact, probably arriving by canoe from Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. After a failed Spanish attempt in 1520, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-warner">Edward Warner</a> founded the first successful British colony in 1632, and Barbuda was long controlled by the Codrington family. Autonomy grew slowly after emancipation in 1834, and the first democratic elections were held in 1951. Antigua and Barbuda gained full independence on 1 November 1981 and remains a Commonwealth realm.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The country covers 440 square kilometres and is mostly flat. Limestone rather than volcanic activity shaped both main islands, and the highest point, Boggy Peak in south-western Antigua, is the remnant of a volcanic crater rising 402 metres. Codrington Lagoon on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a> is one of the largest internal bodies of water in the Lesser Antilles. Rainfall is low and neither island has enough fresh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/redonda">Redonda</a>, a rocky uninhabited island, lies about 40 kilometres south-west of Antigua. Antigua is Spanish for ancient and barbuda for bearded, which is thought to refer either to the male inhabitants of Barbuda or to its bearded fig trees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaic-period hunter-gatherers called the Ciboney were the first culture present, and carbon dating places the earliest settlements around 3700 BCE. They were succeeded by the Arawak-speaking Saladoid people from the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco River</a>, who introduced agriculture, among other crops the Antigua black pineapple, corn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>. The Caribs settled later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> sighted the islands in 1493, but the Spanish did not colonise Antigua until disease, malnutrition and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> had extirpated most of the native population, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> probably killing most.</p><p>English settlers came to Antigua in 1632, and Christopher Codrington settled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a> in 1685. Tobacco and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> were grown by a large population of slaves taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, who soon vastly outnumbered the European settlers. Prince Klaas first proposed independence in 1728, when he attempted to make the island an independent kingdom. After emancipation in 1834 Antigua's autonomy slowly increased, while Barbuda was gradually integrated into it; the Codrington family controlled Barbuda until the 1860s. The first democratic elections were held in 1951.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The most populated city is St. John's, and the only settlement on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a> is Codrington. The country is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> within the Commonwealth, represented by a governor-general, and Barbuda has been administered by the Barbuda Council since 1976. Antigua is divided into six parishes. The population was estimated at 106,365 in 2026, and the most spoken home language is Antiguan and Barbudan Creole. Hurricane Irma, on 6 September 2017, damaged 95 per cent of the structures on Barbuda, and some 1,800 people were evacuated to Antigua.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antigua and Barbuda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
